{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "התנשמת", :translit "ha-tinshemes", :gloss "the tinshemet"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology: root ש-ו-מ meaning 'to be astonished'"}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim reads the bird name התנשמת (Leviticus 11:18) as though it derives from שימה, astonishment, so that the very name of the creature describes the reaction of anyone who beholds it. This is a play on the root ש-ו-מ as in 'נשמו' used for astonishment (Jeremiah 2:12).", :sources ["Leviticus 11:18" "Jeremiah 2:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "הקאת", :translit "ha-ka'as", :gloss "the ka'at"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology from root ק-ו-א, 'to vomit'"}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim reads the bird name הקאת (Leviticus 11:18) as derived from the root ק-ו-א, to vomit, describing the creature's feeding habit. The noun form appears as קֵאָה meaning vomit (Leviticus 18:25, 18:28, 20:22).", :sources ["Leviticus 11:18" "Leviticus 18:25" "Leviticus 20:22"]}} {:quotes [{:he "הרחם", :translit "ha-racham", :gloss "the racham (bird of prey)"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology from root ר-ח-מ, 'to have compassion'"}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim reads the bird name הרחם (Leviticus 11:18) as derived from רחמים, compassion, because this species is said to display unusual devotion to its young. The word רחם as a bird name appears only in the list of non-kosher birds (Leviticus 11:18, Deuteronomy 14:17).", :sources ["Leviticus 11:18" "Deuteronomy 14:17"]}} {:quotes [{:he "עורב למינו", :translit "orev le-mino", :gloss "raven after its kind"} {:he "זרזר", :translit "zarzir", :gloss "starling"} {:he "שרצים", :translit "sheratzim", :gloss "swarming creatures"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "עורב למינו", :b "זרזר"} {:kind :other, :note "Sheratzim category left universally forbidden because serpent is among them"}], :frame {:text "The gematria equating עורב למינו with זרזר identifies an additional species included under the raven type. Regarding שרצים, Leviticus 11:29-30 and 11:41-42 enumerate specific and general sheratzim as forbidden; the serpent is explicitly cursed among animals in Genesis 3:14.", :sources ["Leviticus 11:14" "Genesis 3:14" "Leviticus 11:29" "Leviticus 11:41"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology: root ש-ו-מ meaning 'to be astonished'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology from root ק-ו-א, 'to vomit'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Name explained via folk etymology from root ר-ח-מ, 'to have compassion'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "עורב למינו", :a-value 414, :b "זרזר", :b-value 414, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Sheratzim category left universally forbidden because serpent is among them", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:23:11.870196659Z", :model "glm-5.2"}